      <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>

      <h3>What is aerosol manufacturing and how does it work?</h3>
      <p>Aerosol manufacturing involves filling a pressure-rated container with a formulated product and a propellant that generates controlled spray, foam, or jet discharge when the actuator is pressed. Internal pressure drives the product through the valve and actuator in a defined pattern.</p>

      <h3>What investment is required to start an aerosol manufacturing plant?</h3>
      <p>Investment depends on desired capacity, automation level, and safety infrastructure. A typical plant must budget for explosion-proof filling lines, gas storage and handling systems, fire protection, quality control laboratories, and regulatory approvals.</p>

      <h3>What are the most commonly used aerosol propellants?</h3>
      <p>Common propellants include propane, n-butane, isobutane, dimethyl ether, carbon dioxide, nitrogen, and nitrous oxide for food applications.</p>

      <h3>Is aerosol manufacturing legally regulated in India and globally?</h3>
      <p>Yes. In India it is regulated by explosives and petroleum authorities, factory and fire departments, and legal metrology authorities. Internationally, aerosol products must comply with UN and DOT transport rules, GHS labeling, and VOC regulations.</p>

      <h3>What are the main safety risks in aerosol manufacturing?</h3>
      <p>The main risks are flammability, explosion due to over-pressurization, gas leaks, static ignition, and improper storage of filled aerosols. These are mitigated through careful plant design, monitoring, and operating procedures.</p>

      <h3>What is the difference between liquefied gas and compressed gas systems?</h3>
      <p>Liquefied gas systems maintain nearly constant pressure as the product is discharged, while compressed gas systems show a gradual drop in pressure during use.</p>

      <h3>How is aerosol product stability tested?</h3>
      <p>Stability is evaluated using accelerated aging, freeze–thaw cycles, pressure retention measurements, spray performance testing, and corrosion and compatibility studies.</p>

      <h3>Which aerosol products are most profitable?</h3>
      <p>Automotive aerosols, household cleaners, personal care products, and private-label aerosols are often profitable segments, depending on market positioning and cost structure.</p>

      <h3>Can aerosol manufacturing be outsourced?</h3>
      <p>Yes. OEM and private-label manufacturing are commonly used to enter the market without building a full plant initially.</p>

